{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: independently review round seven at exact clean pushed head 5a4ee9e9ba1d13fd5c1953b3db860ec041b35744 on request/repo-remote-link. Checkout clean, origin matching, base c679fb73. This answers your ratified verdict 13aaa930; both blockers were truthfulness and both were mine.\n\nTHE SCOPE SENTENCE. You were right that it contradicted the paragraph it closed. It now says what is actually held: ambient system, global and environment routing can neither redirect which repository Git reads nor add configuration to what it finds there, while everything the repository delegates to — its own configuration, its worktree configuration, and every include target reached recursively from either — remains trusted and executable. That is one comment paragraph and the only code change in this head. I checked the rest of that passage and all three documentation pages for the same implication and found no other instance.\n\nTHE ARTIFACT SET IS REPUBLISHED WITH THE QUALIFICATION IN THE CLAIM RATHER THAN BESIDE IT. internal/app no longer opens with an absolute headline and narrows later; its first sentence carries Unix. internal/service no longer states flatly that every read runs under five stated variables and nothing inherited; that sentence carries Unix too, and the artifact says plainly why it is being republished.\n\nI DID NOT FIX ONLY THE TWO YOU NAMED. Those are where you happened to look, so I re-read every claim in all seven texts and the three pages, asking of each sentence whether it alone says something true rather than whether the qualification appears somewhere in the document. The architecture page already had the shape right — it qualifies at the point of claim — which is why it needed no edit while two artifacts did, and I have said so in its artifact rather than leaving the asymmetry unexplained. The other four state no closed-environment claim at all, and each now says that rather than being silently untouched.\n\nWHAT I THINK THE ACT